WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.580
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:10.630
Not so long ago, OpenAI has released GPT-2,
an AI that was trained to look at a piece

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:16.390
of text and perform common natural language
processing operations on it, for instance,

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:19.960
answering questions, summarization, and more.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:25.430
But today, we are going to be laser focused
on only one of these tasks.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:31.260
And that task is continuation, where we give
an AI a bunch of text and we ask it to continue

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:32.260
it.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:37.290
However, as these learning algorithms are
quite general by design, here comes the twist

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.950
- who said that this can only work for text?

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.090
Why not try it on composing music?

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:49.770
So let’s have a look at some results, where
only the first 6 notes were given from a song,

00:00:49.770 --> 00:01:06.360
and the AI was asked to continue it.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:35.630
Love it.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:40.190
This is a great testament to the power of
general learning algorithms.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:44.590
As you’ve heard, this works great for a
variety of different genres as well, and not

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:49.689
only that, but it can also create really cool
blends between genres.

00:01:49.689 --> 00:01:55.369
Listen as the AI starts out from the first
6 notes of a Chopin piece and transitions

00:01:55.369 --> 00:02:00.010
into a pop style with a bunch of different
instruments entering a few seconds in.

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:20.919
And, great news, because if you look here,
we can try our own combinations through an

00:02:20.919 --> 00:02:22.730
online demo as well.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:28.200
On the left side, we can specify and hear
the short input sample, and ask for a variety

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.989
of different styles for the continuation.

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:35.749
It is amazing fun, try it, I’ve put a link
in the video description.

00:02:35.749 --> 00:02:53.029
I was particularly impressed with this combination.

00:02:53.029 --> 00:02:54.029
Really cool.

00:02:54.029 --> 00:03:00.159
Now, this algorithm is also not without limitations
as it has difficulties pairing instruments

00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:05.519
that either don’t go too well together or
there is lacking training data on how they

00:03:05.519 --> 00:03:07.319
should sound together.

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:12.900
The source code is also either already available
as of the publishing of this video, or will

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.359
be available soon.

00:03:14.359 --> 00:03:18.279
If so, I will come back and update the video
description with the link.

00:03:18.279 --> 00:03:24.239
OpenAI has also published an almost two-hour
concert with ton of different genres so make

00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:27.120
sure to head to the video description and
check it out yourself!

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.650
I think these techniques are either already
so powerful, or will soon be powerful enough

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:37.219
to raise important copyright questions, and
we’ll need plenty of discussions on who

00:03:37.219 --> 00:03:39.109
really owns this piece of music.

00:03:39.109 --> 00:03:40.909
What do you think?

00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:42.159
Let me know in the comments!

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:46.059
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

